<h2>A short paragraph with real weight</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and commercial property policies sold in Idaho include a condition called “Appraisal.” The wording varies by company, but the structure is familiar: if you and the insurer fail to agree on the amount of loss, either side may demand appraisal in writing. Each side picks a competent and impartial appraiser. Those two choose an umpire. An itemized decision signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>That is a narrow job, and it is meant to be. The panel answers the question “what does it cost to put this right?” It does not rewrite the policy or decide whether a peril is covered. Inside that lane, though, the result carries real force, so the person you appoint has to be able to defend every line.</p>
<h2>What appraisal clause representation includes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deadline check on day one.</strong> Some forms give each side 20 days to name an appraiser after a written demand. We read your form and the demand letter before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>Our own inspection.</strong> We measure, photograph and scope the property ourselves. We do not start from the carrier’s estimate and nudge the numbers.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item valuation</strong> priced for your part of Idaho, whether that is a Meridian tract home, a Coeur d’Alene lake cabin or a potato cellar outside Rexburg.</li>
<li><strong>Working sessions with the other appraiser.</strong> Most of the gap usually closes here, one category at a time.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation</strong> for anything left unresolved, with photos, measurements and pricing support for each item.</li>
<li><strong>Award review</strong> so replacement cost, actual cash value and any holdback are written clearly before signatures go on the page.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep in mind</b>Appraisal sets a number. It does not decide coverage, award attorney fees or resolve bad-faith questions. If your claim was denied outright, speak with an Idaho attorney before you consider appraisal.</div>
<h2>Signs your claim is ready for it</h2>
<p>Representation fits when the insurer has accepted at least part of the loss and the disagreement is about scope or price: how many roof planes hail actually reached, whether the snow-load damage extends to the trusses, what it costs to replace matching siding in Ada County this season. It fits poorly when the real dispute is an exclusion, a late-notice defense or a question of what the policy means.</p>
<h2>How we stay credible with the panel</h2>
<p>An umpire listens to the appraiser whose numbers hold together. We price what we can document, and we tell you early which items are strong and which are thin. That honesty is what moves an award; an inflated opening figure usually does the opposite.</p>

<h2>Questions about appraisal clause representation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can my roofing contractor be my appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most policies require an impartial or disinterested appraiser. Someone who stands to earn the repair contract can give the insurer grounds to challenge the award, so it is usually wiser to keep those roles separate.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the insurer won’t name its appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many appraisal clauses let either party ask a court to appoint an umpire, and some address a missing appraiser. If the other side stalls, an Idaho attorney can explain the options under your specific policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A straightforward residential claim often wraps up within two to three months after both appraisers are named. Commercial losses, large contents claims and files that need an umpire hearing take longer.</p></div></details></div>
